WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.560
if you live in a relatively large European city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:06.640
it's very likely that you've seen a&nbsp;
company like Getir, Flink, or Gorillas.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.080
What these companies do is pretty simple:&nbsp;
deliver you your groceries very quickly.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.120
But, if you're paying a bit more attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.640
you might also ask yourself how they've&nbsp;
managed to be practically everywhere.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.040
I see fast grocery delivery writers&nbsp;
every single time I go outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.520
and I see their marketing&nbsp;
on practically everything.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.280
Even Gorillas, recognizable&nbsp;
for their pitch black outfits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.560
didn't exist one and a half years&nbsp;
ago, and it has already become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.520
Europe's fastest growing unicorn –ever –&nbsp;
surpassing a valuation of one billion dollars.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.880
The rise of these companies begs two&nbsp;
simple questions why are they so valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.840
and why is Europe particularly&nbsp;
dominant in producing them.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.360
You, an average consumer, lazy that you are,

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.520
face multiple costs beyond the sticker&nbsp;
price you see while buying groceries.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.720
If i wanted to go to the grocery&nbsp;
store right now, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.840
I would have to grab my keys from the&nbsp;
shelf at the other end of the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.560
lock my door, walk to the store, make painful&nbsp;
eye contact with people on the street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.280
look for my food in the store, and then I have to&nbsp;
walk back home again, make eye contact with people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.960
on the street, again, unlock my doors, put my&nbsp;
keys back on the shelf, and then prepare a meal.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.840
All of these actions require something from me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.600
so it's understandable how these fast&nbsp;
grocery delivery companies have managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.560
to make a somewhat sustainable business by&nbsp;
promising me to cut these extra costs out.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.720
These companies know that we'll practically&nbsp;
always have our phones on us, so instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.280
of facing the travel costs you would normally&nbsp;
need to experience, all you need to do instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.200
is open up their app, type the basic&nbsp;
consumer good you want in the search bar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.200
face a more than manageable markup (the cost&nbsp;
they add up as a company beyond the sticker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.720
price they initially paid for their&nbsp;
goods) and then wait for it to arrive.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.560
In many cases, the food will arrive faster&nbsp;
than what it would have taken for you to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.880
it yourself: the value proposition&nbsp;
these companies have makes sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.600
but it's much less clear if&nbsp;
their extreme valuations do.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.080
Getir, the turkey-based company&nbsp;
that's currently leading the pack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.080
has a 7.5 billion dollar valuation and closed&nbsp;
around 1.1 billion dollars in fundraising.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.240
Gorillas, the german-based company, has&nbsp;
received outside funding from investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.640
like the Chinese gaming giant Tencent&nbsp;
and also has a billion dollar valuation.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.240
Flink, the third largest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.640
has raised hundreds of millions of euros&nbsp;
in funds – 240 million to be exact – and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.800
multiple other delivery startups that I haven't&nbsp;
even mentioned have also received millions.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:31.920
The fact that these companies have seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.920
massive inflows of capital pretty&nbsp;
much explains the entire story.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.400
There's practically no way that there could be&nbsp;
so many of these fast delivery companies in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.000
same city that are offering their products&nbsp;
for only a fraction more than the regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.040
prices in the supermarket while also burning&nbsp;
a ton of capital on promotion and marketing.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.040
The only reason they can offer such a&nbsp;
cheap service while excessively advertising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.280
is because they're all insulated by&nbsp;
large amounts of venture capital funding.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.440
If i walk around my city, I'll have a near&nbsp;
guarantee that i'll see a tram covered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.600
Getir branding, and I'll see plenty of&nbsp;
bikers with the corresponding attire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.840
with all different, but recognizable colors.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.800
The exposure to the excessive branding reveals&nbsp;
a deeper level of competition under the water.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.480
In order to crowd the others out, you're&nbsp;
going to see the use of any mechanism that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.560
can attract the most consumers fastest:&nbsp;
pushing their laborers to the limit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.560
downward pressure on the price that they charge,&nbsp;
and a serious amount of marketing and promotion.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.520
The reason the marketing and&nbsp;
promotion is so important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.840
is because the main products these companies&nbsp;
provide, alongside their delivery services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.440
are relatively homogeneous with&nbsp;
little to no room for differentiation.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.000
It's quite unlikely that people will&nbsp;
have multiple fast grocery delivery apps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.640
when each delivery app provides essentially&nbsp;
the same not really differentiable goods:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.560
a crate of heineken, some dish soap or ben&nbsp;
and jerry's ice cream when it's convenient.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.040
What the companies really want is for their&nbsp;
app to be the first one you actually download,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.880
and venture capitalists know that it's a&nbsp;
matter of betting on the right horse that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.240
can weather the storm. With the storm&nbsp;
being the other delivery companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.600
so they'll load up the brands with enough&nbsp;
funding to the point that the individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.440
company can still operate at a relative&nbsp;
loss while the others inevitably lose

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.320
Gorillas has already been&nbsp;
compared with Wework – the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.200
poster child for an overvalued startup in the US.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.720
They've faced a lot of criticism for&nbsp;
pushing their gig workers extremely hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.360
and they've also received&nbsp;
hundreds of millions in funding.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.400
The high amount of flowing capital&nbsp;
has been partially attributed to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.160
presence of Gorilla's charismatic CEO&nbsp;
who seems to be religiously concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.760
with spending money to scale before&nbsp;
getting the revenue stream to match.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:39.040
Gorilas predicted an annual reoccurring revenue&nbsp;
of 2.6 billion dollars by the end of 2021;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.280
however, in April of this year, its&nbsp;
annual recurring revenue was only 196&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.160
million – a steep uphill climb is necessary&nbsp;
to meet their own self-laid expectations.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.640
We can't see their consistent revenue figures&nbsp;
right now because they're a private company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:56.240
but i'm willing to bet that they're still&nbsp;
pretty shy of 2.6 billion dollars in revenue.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.200
It's not to say that these companies make&nbsp;
miscalculations on purpose or that gorillas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.120
couldn't meet that revenue eventually but it&nbsp;
shows that the priorities of these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.840
are scale, scale, and again scale before&nbsp;
actual predictable revenue is produced.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.800
Getir, Gorillas, and Flink, are&nbsp;
all European-based companies.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.200
Although the US may still have unicorns that&nbsp;
are just as valuable or even more valuable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.720
the speed at which these European firms&nbsp;
have grown is particularly relevant.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.320
Although a lot of Americans like to&nbsp;
think that the reason they have more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:30.560
globally successful companies is due&nbsp;
to a bunch of individual factors most,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.040
europeans know the giant elephant in the&nbsp;
room the US simply has a larger market.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.280
It's not so much a question of more or less&nbsp;
inherent skill as much as it is a question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.680
of a more or less unified consumer base,&nbsp;
which the US has while Europe does not.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:48.800
So, when European food tech companies grow&nbsp;
faster than their American counterparts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.320
that requires a bit more scrutiny.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:55.120
An obvious reason for why European&nbsp;
food tech unicorns can grow so fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.960
is because of the playing field they're operating.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.920
Compared to other places Europe is simply&nbsp;
ahead with public infrastructure like bike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.240
lanes that provide ample, more affordable&nbsp;
opportunities in building a delivery force.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.000
This stands in clear contrast&nbsp;
with delivery services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.320
that are primarily reliant on cars for deliveries.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.640
Where cities in america may currently&nbsp;
have difficulty adopting cargo bikes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.880
it's already the norm in many European cities.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.400
It's safe to say that the playing field for fast&nbsp;
grocery delivery companies will thin eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.360
so treat the app you install as almost a vote&nbsp;
for which one you actually want to survive.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.320
It's impressive how much these companies have&nbsp;
grown, but it's also not entirely a surprise.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.720
The venture capital funding landscape of Europe&nbsp;
is growing tremendously and the rise of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:47.840
European giants is a reflection of a development&nbsp;
we may have to get used to in the future.

